{"text": "Category B is Deafblind. The educational description is: A student whose combined visual and auditory impairments create significant difficulty developing communication, educational, vocational, recreational, and social skills. Either impairment may range from partial to profound. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Mateo Singh, who is in Grade 4 and uses he/him pronouns. Mateo has congenital deafblindness with limited usable vision and hearing. He learns at grade level when information is presented through tactile and accessible formats. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Mateo, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Mateo’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-B-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.7125, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "B", "start": 0.725, "end": 1.075, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.0875, "end": 1.2875, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Deafblind", "start": 1.3, "end": 2.1375, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "The", "start": 2.5, "end": 2.6875, "start_char": 25, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "educational", "start": 2.7, "end": 3.3375, "start_char": 29, "end_char": 40}, {"text": "description", "start": 3.35, "end": 3.9, "start_char": 41, "end_char": 52}